Subject: [PATCH] relax the MinGW guard so the Qt editor can be built with it

juce_TargetPlatform.h refuses MinGW outright.  That refusal is about JUCE's
own audio backends; this project's Qt editor is a separate front end and can
be built with Qt's MinGW toolchain, which is what
Config/cmake/toolchain-mingw64.cmake sets up.

The guard is replaced with JUCE_MINGW=1 and nothing else in the header
changes, so a MinGW build compiles the code JUCE already has for that
platform instead of stopping at the #error.

Kept as a patch over the submodule rather than a fork so the pinned upstream
tag stays readable.
---
--- a/modules/juce_core/system/juce_TargetPlatform.h
+++ b/modules/juce_core/system/juce_TargetPlatform.h
@@ -110,7 +110,10 @@
   #endif
 
   #ifdef __MINGW32__
-    #error "MinGW is not supported. Please use an alternative compiler."
+    // Upstream JUCE refuses MinGW.  Overteon builds the Qt editor with
+    // Qt's own MinGW toolchain (see Config/cmake/toolchain-mingw64.cmake),
+    // so the guard is relaxed here.  Nothing else in this header changes.
+    #define JUCE_MINGW 1
   #endif
 
   /** If defined, this indicates that the processor is little-endian. */
